WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.969
Now before we get started on the displacement part

2
00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:06.969
of our landscape material,

3
00:00:06.969 --> 00:00:11.289
I wanna talk really quick about how this works.

4
00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:13.849
First off, in order to actually use this,

5
00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:15.330
you are going to need to go

6
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.210
to the details panel of your material

7
00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:18.809
and then under Nanite,

8
00:00:18.809 --> 00:00:21.570
enable the enable tessellation checkmark box.

9
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:25.210
That's going to enable the displacement input

10
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.070
on your material.

11
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.449
Now under displacement in the details panel,

12
00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:33.009
there's also a magnitude and a center.

13
00:00:33.009 --> 00:00:38.009
What these two properties do is control the steepness

14
00:00:38.409 --> 00:00:42.990
and kind of the center or the high and the low points

15
00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.649
of your displacement.

16
00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:47.090
So for example, if we were to take the center

17
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.929
and set this to a value of one,

18
00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:51.770
what would end up happening

19
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.969
is we'd get something like this far image,

20
00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:58.009
the entire displacement would be actually shifted up.

21
00:00:58.009 --> 00:01:02.130
As we start to move the center down the,

22
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.450
from say a value of one to 0.5,

23
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:05.730
all the way down to 0.1,

24
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.390
we're actually gonna be shifting

25
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.810
or offsetting the displacement.

26
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.569
So here in the middle, we have a value of 0.5

27
00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:16.370
and then here we have a value of one

28
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:20.269
and the 0.5 value looks and works pretty well

29
00:01:20.269 --> 00:01:22.879
with this content.

30
00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:24.959
The magnitude on the other hand,

31
00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:29.200
this is gonna control the steepness of your displacement.

32
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.519
So if we have a low magnitude,

33
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:33.480
we'll have kind of high bumps,

34
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.900
kind of not really that big.

35
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.159
If we start to increase the magnitude really,

36
00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:38.379
really, really big,

37
00:01:38.379 --> 00:01:42.159
then we're gonna get some really, really large features.

38
00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:46.280
We can also control our displacement through our material

39
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.019
by multiplying our end displacement

40
00:01:49.019 --> 00:01:51.959
by some value to control over that.

41
00:01:51.959 --> 00:01:54.019
So for our material,

42
00:01:54.019 --> 00:01:56.060
what I have found throughout testing and building this

43
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.219
is that if we set the magnitude to 1.0

44
00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:00.540
and leave the center at 0.5,

45
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.540
with the content, we will get really good results.

46
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.760
So let's go ahead and pop back over to our material now.

47
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.580
So right here, and then we get to click out of,

48
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:10.939
have nothing selected,

49
00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:11.900
and then we're gonna come in here

50
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.460
and we're gonna look for our Nanite

51
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.620
and then here, enable tessellation.

52
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.620
And you can see here, I get the displacement input

53
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.300
and then right here under our displacement,

54
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.039
we're gonna set our magnitude to a value of one

55
00:02:22.039 --> 00:02:25.979
and hit apply and then save.

56
00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:28.680
And what we're gonna be doing inside of our material now

57
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.659
is the following.

58
00:02:29.659 --> 00:02:34.689
We're going to basically take our dirt and our grass,

59
00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:36.389
copy them.

60
00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.629
We're gonna change the channel though to the blue channel

61
00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:40.389
because that is where the displacement

62
00:02:40.389 --> 00:02:43.750
is located inside of our mask.

63
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.949
And unlike our other ones, at the very end of our lerp,

64
00:02:46.949 --> 00:02:48.849
before we go into our displacement,

65
00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:51.949
we are gonna add a multiply and then a displacement amount

66
00:02:51.949 --> 00:02:54.650
to allow us to control that displacement

67
00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:55.789
inside of our material.

68
00:02:55.789 --> 00:02:58.189
So let's pop back here to the editor now.

69
00:02:58.189 --> 00:02:59.030
There we go.

70
00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.710
So we'll just take this grass tile,

71
00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.210
take all this code right up here,

72
00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.069
control C, control V, move this down,

73
00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:08.270
and then we're gonna rename this to D grass

74
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:09.789
for displacement grass,

75
00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:13.090
and then D dirt for displacement dirt.

76
00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:14.949
And we do need to change these textures.

77
00:03:14.949 --> 00:03:16.990
So that's just a matter of coming in here

78
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.990
and selecting our grass

79
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.969
and then doing that again for this one,

80
00:03:20.969 --> 00:03:23.030
coming in here and selecting our rock.

81
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:23.870
There we go.

82
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.129
And then we're gonna change this to the blue pen.

83
00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:30.759
So right there and then right there like so.

84
00:03:30.759 --> 00:03:34.020
And now we need to add our displacement controls.

85
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.479
So we're gonna hold down the one key and click,

86
00:03:36.479 --> 00:03:37.719
and we're gonna right click on this,

87
00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:43.680
convert to parameter and call this displacement amount.

88
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.060
And we're gonna give this a value of one.

89
00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.919
And then to control this,

90
00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:49.099
we're gonna hold down the M key.

91
00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:50.439
We're gonna add a multiply,

92
00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:53.599
and then we're going to have our displacement value

93
00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:54.520
go into here,

94
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.319
and then we're gonna have this go into our multiply

95
00:03:56.319 --> 00:03:57.159
like so.

96
00:03:57.159 --> 00:03:58.960
And then last but not least,

97
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.780
we're gonna come right up to our displacement

98
00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:07.159
and hit apply and save.

99
00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:09.360
All right, fantastic.

100
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.060
So what we're gonna be doing next

101
00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.039
is we're gonna be adding something called distance blending.

102
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:18.639
Now here is an example of distance blending in action.

103
00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:23.040
So this is the exact same location.

104
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.740
And what it's using here on the left hand side

105
00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:31.740
is a texture that maps the entire resolution

106
00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.560
of the landscape to one texture.

107
00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.319
And while this is very performant

108
00:04:36.319 --> 00:04:38.620
and looks really good for the detail

109
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:39.980
and the far distance,

110
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.959
the detail that's very close to the camera

111
00:04:41.959 --> 00:04:44.879
and the mid ground doesn't really look all that great.

112
00:04:44.879 --> 00:04:46.079
It's actually very pixelated.

113
00:04:46.079 --> 00:04:49.319
We can actually even see the individual texels

114
00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:50.680
inside of that texture map,

115
00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.139
and that's just not very good.

116
00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:55.379
On the right hand side,

117
00:04:55.379 --> 00:04:58.040
we have the exact same setup,

118
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.459
but we have this distance blending enabled.

119
00:05:00.459 --> 00:05:02.259
And what distance blending allows us to do

120
00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:05.899
is it allows us to basically take two different textures

121
00:05:05.899 --> 00:05:09.180
and have one texture displayed close to the camera

122
00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.019
and in the mid ground,

123
00:05:10.019 --> 00:05:12.100
and then the other texture displayed

124
00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.740
far off in the distance.

125
00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.860
And we're actually gonna utilize this

126
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.019
for our landscape material

127
00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:22.660
by creating the following material distance

128
00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:23.939
blending function.

129
00:05:23.939 --> 00:05:26.540
So what we're gonna do is we're gonna create a parameter

130
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.060
and we're gonna call that distance blend.

131
00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.660
And then there is a material function

132
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.779
called distance blend.

133
00:05:31.779 --> 00:05:32.939
We're gonna use that

134
00:05:32.939 --> 00:05:36.019
and plug that into our distance blend reroute.

135
00:05:36.019 --> 00:05:39.199
So let's go ahead and do that now.

136
00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:40.639
So inside of our material,

137
00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:43.860
we're gonna just come over here to some area

138
00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:44.939
and I'm gonna hold down again,

139
00:05:44.939 --> 00:05:46.959
the one key on the keyboard and click.

140
00:05:46.959 --> 00:05:49.779
We're gonna click on this and go convert to parameter.

141
00:05:49.779 --> 00:05:52.720
We're gonna call this distance blend range.

142
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.100
We're gonna give this a default value of say 1000.

143
00:05:58.139 --> 00:05:59.459
And then what we're gonna do

144
00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:02.100
is we're gonna look for our distance blend

145
00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.980
by clicking into our graph.

146
00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.560
And there we go, miss distance blend.

147
00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.480
And then we're gonna take the output of this range

148
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.339
and plug that right here like so.

149
00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:14.740
And then what we're gonna do

150
00:06:14.779 --> 00:06:17.860
is we are going to click inside of here

151
00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.420
and we're gonna look for a named

152
00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:24.060
and we're gonna add a named reroute declaration node.

153
00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.550
And we're gonna call that distance blend.

154
00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.509
And then we're gonna plug this in like so.

155
00:06:31.509 --> 00:06:32.350
So there we go.

156
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.509
Just have a look at this and we'll recap this.

157
00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:36.990
What we did is we took that distance blend.

158
00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.370
We made a parameter by holding down one

159
00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.069
and left clicking to add the constant.

160
00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:43.750
Then we right clicked on it, converted it to a parameter,

161
00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.670
gave it a name of distance blend range

162
00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.769
and a default value of 1000.

163
00:06:47.769 --> 00:06:50.230
We then right clicked inside of our event graph

164
00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.949
to look for our distance blend function.

165
00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:56.990
And this allows us again to set that range.

166
00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.129
We connected our output here

167
00:06:59.129 --> 00:07:02.269
and then we added our named reroute declaration,

168
00:07:02.269 --> 00:07:05.800
called it distance blend and plug these in like so.

169
00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.120
All right, so we are going to need

170
00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.100
to add some other commands here.

171
00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:16.800
We are going to make a far tile grass and a far tile dirt.

172
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.459
And they're basically going to be exactly the same

173
00:07:20.459 --> 00:07:23.180
as our dirt tile and our grass tile.

174
00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:24.939
This is just gonna be for things in the difference.

175
00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:26.779
And the main difference is that again,

176
00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:27.800
we're gonna change the name,

177
00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:29.779
but we're also gonna change the default value

178
00:07:29.779 --> 00:07:31.459
to be something very, very small.

179
00:07:31.459 --> 00:07:33.860
And this is gonna make the texture really large.

180
00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.199
Let's go ahead and do that now.

181
00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:37.660
So we're gonna come over here

182
00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:41.500
and let's just grab these two nodes right here

183
00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.220
and we can control C, control V

184
00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.660
and we'll just move them out of the way.

185
00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.500
And then what we're gonna call this

186
00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.139
is we're just gonna put in the word, F-A-R underscore.

187
00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.019
What I'm actually gonna do is I'm gonna take this farm

188
00:07:55.019 --> 00:07:58.759
to copy it to my clipboard here.

189
00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:00.819
And then we're just gonna do this.

190
00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:02.920
We're gonna come here and we're gonna go paste.

191
00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:04.779
So we get that far dirt tile.

192
00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:06.459
Oops, need that E there.

193
00:08:06.459 --> 00:08:08.579
And then we're gonna do the same thing for here,

194
00:08:08.579 --> 00:08:09.920
grass tile.

195
00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.689
So we're gonna call this far grass tile.

196
00:08:14.689 --> 00:08:20.639
And then we're gonna do the same thing here.

197
00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:21.480
So there we go.

198
00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.360
We have now a far dirt tile

199
00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.709
and our far grass tile with the company nodes.

200
00:08:28.709 --> 00:08:33.509
And now what we're gonna do is we are going to grab,

201
00:08:33.509 --> 00:08:37.429
basically set up our same slope like we have,

202
00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:40.830
but instead of it being dirt tile or grass tile,

